\section{The Tree Between Keystrokes}

A batch compiler can stop at the first syntax error. An editor needs to know what surrounds the error: which scope contains the cursor, whether the next declaration still exists, and which language owns a particular span. Lukas Diekmann's \textit{Editing composed languages} describes an editor built around incremental trees and dedicated language boxes. Each box holds a nested parse tree maintained by its own parser. Diekmann's approach to history-based recovery preserves usable structure across edits rather than requiring every intermediate line of text to be legal. A recovered tree is still not evidence that the current text is a valid program.

A frontend that serves an editor should make that distinction visible in its data. A concrete tree can represent unexpected real tokens and synthetic missing ones separately. Real tokens retain their spans, even if the normal grammar did not use them; synthetic tokens have an insertion point, not a span of source to delete. Preserve the actual text underneath both valid and damaged regions. A formatter may decline to format the damaged subtree; highlighting and name lookup can still use neighboring valid subtrees.

\section{Worked Edit: A Hole Inside an Embedded Language}

Suppose a report language embeds arithmetic queries between \texttt{[[} and \texttt{]]}. Start with the following valid report, and delete only the second occurrence of \texttt{price}:
\begin{verbatim}
query [[price + price]];
show total;
\end{verbatim}
The nested parser now encounters its box boundary when it expects an operand. It should report the missing operand at the end of the query and can return an addition node with a missing right child. The host parser still sees the end of the box and the statement terminator, so \texttt{show total;} does not disappear into the error. In this particular syntax the host owns the box boundary; it can pass a bounded token stream to the query parser, which cannot accidentally consume tokens from the following statement.

Anthony Aaby's \textit{Compiler Construction using Flex and Bison} illustrates another mechanism: a grammar production using the special \texttt{error} token can synchronize at a semicolon or a closing delimiter, discarding parser context and tokens as needed. The idea transfers to an editor, but discarded source should remain visible as an error region rather than vanish from the document model. The same synchronization token can be appropriate in one grammar and dangerous in another. A semicolon nested inside a query string is not necessarily the end of a host statement. If the user deletes the closing box delimiter, use the language's explicit boundary policy, a marked provisional span, or a bounded fallback rather than giving the nested parser unrestricted ownership of the file.

\section{When Reuse Is Sound}

Unchanged source text is necessary for direct reuse of a token node, but does not make its context independent. For example, inserting a quote before a token can change the lexer mode in which the token was recognized. Similarly, a subtree that started in one parser state may not be reusable in another. A cache needs to check the relevant entry conditions, including lexer modes, parser context, and the grammar or extension configuration. For valid text, the incrementally maintained tree should agree with a fresh parse at the same revision, up to identity and metadata such as reuse counters. For incomplete text, a history-based method may intentionally choose a different provisional tree; test instead that it covers source faithfully, resolves or flags uncertainty, and does not cross ownership boundaries.

A useful edit script deletes the operand, inserts it back, deletes the box terminator, and undoes both edits. Test that recovery makes progress on every step: it consumes input, unwinds to a different bounded recovery context, or spends from a finite insertion budget. Test that restoring validity removes the synthetic node and its diagnostic. The parser can reuse a nearby tree node yet still need to recheck bindings after an environment change; see Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-133}. \href{https://github.com/Chubek/TheTomeOfLangDev/blob/HEAD/dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/22-incremental-compiler-as-a-reactive-system.md}{Markdown Dossier Chapter~22} generalizes this principle to dependencies across the incremental frontend.
